    <h1 class="rv in" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,4rem);max-width:20ch">How Do You Book a Surprise Boat Trip in Madeira Without Spoiling It?</h1>
    <div class="artmeta rv in d1"><span>2 January 2027</span><span>6 min read</span><span>Funchal · Madeira</span></div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="pad">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="article rv">
      <p class="lede">Book it in your own name, keep every confirmation and message on your phone, and give the other person only two clues: what to wear and what time to leave the hotel. Getting them to the pontoon at Marina do Funchal without a hint is the part you plan yourself.</p>

      <h2>Whose name and number should go on the booking?</h2><p>Yours, and only yours. The booking confirmation, the payment receipt and any WhatsApp messages about the trip go to the person who booked. If that is you, the secret stays on your phone.</p><p>The leaks usually come from somewhere else. Check these before you book:</p><ul><li><strong>A shared email account or a family calendar</strong> that copies bookings automatically.</li><li><strong>Booking app notifications</strong> that pop up on a lock screen at breakfast. Turn previews off for a few days.</li><li><strong>A shared card</strong> whose statement or banking app the other person reads.</li><li><strong>Travel companions</strong> who know. Tell as few people as you can, and tell them late.</li></ul><p>You can book a Chifbay trip directly on chifbay.com or through GetYourGuide, Viator or Click&amp;Boat. Whichever you use, it is the account holder who gets the messages, so use your own account.</p><h2>Which trip makes the better surprise?</h2><p>The <a href="/sunset-cruise">Sunset Trip</a>, most of the time. An evening out needs no special clothes, so the cover story writes itself: a drink on the seafront, a dinner reservation, a walk. There is no swimming on it, which means nothing to pack that would give the game away.</p><p>The <a href="/hidden-coves-half-day">Day Trip</a> is harder to hide, but bigger when it lands. It includes two swims, at Fajã dos Padres and off Ribeira Brava, plus paddle boards and the chance to jump in. The other person needs swimwear, which takes a little more invention.</p><p>Check the departure time on your booking rather than guessing. Day Trips have two fixed slots, 10:00 and 14:00. Sunset Trips are set by the sun, departing an hour and a quarter before it goes down, so in winter it can start before 17:00 and in midsummer not until about 20:00. Build your cover story around the real time.</p><h2>What do you tell them to wear and bring?</h2><p>Enough to be comfortable, not enough to guess. Phrase it as part of the cover story.</p><h3>For the Sunset Trip</h3><ul><li><strong>Flat shoes with some grip.</strong> The pontoon and the deck are no place for heels. "We might walk a lot tonight" covers it.</li><li><strong>A warm layer.</strong> The evening breeze on the water feels cooler than the promenade, even in summer. "It gets cool by the sea later" is true and harmless.</li><li><strong>Sunglasses.</strong> The low sun on the way out is bright.</li></ul><h3>For the Day Trip</h3><ul><li><strong>Swimwear under their clothes.</strong> A hotel pool, a beach afternoon or a spa are all good excuses.</li><li><strong>Sun cream, a hat and a towel.</strong> These fit almost any daytime plan.</li><li><strong>A dry top for the way back.</strong> Wet hair at speed gets cold.</li></ul><p>They do not need food, drinks or a camera. Food and drinks are on board, and every trip includes photos taken with a real camera, a 360° video and, when the weather allows, drone footage. Our <a href="/posts/what-to-pack-for-a-boat-trip-in-madeira">packing checklist for a boat trip</a> has the full list for you, the one who knows.</p><h2>How do you get them to the marina without a clue?</h2><p>Walk there as if you were going somewhere else. The marina is right on Funchal's promenade, halfway along it, so walking there looks like any evening stroll. You are just passing the boats on the way to your "dinner".</p><p>A few practical points:</p><ol><li><strong>Leave with time to spare.</strong> Plan to reach the boat 15 minutes early. Your skipper will be waiting beside it.</li><li><strong>Know the walk in advance.</strong> If you can, walk it alone on an earlier day, so you do not hesitate on the way down to the pontoons and give the plan away.</li><li><strong>If you take a taxi</strong>, ask for the seafront rather than the marina, then walk the last part.</li><li><strong>The reveal is on the pontoon.</strong> Until then, it is a walk past some boats. When the skipper welcomes you aboard, it becomes the surprise.</li></ol><h2>Which cover stories work best in Funchal?</h2><p>The ones that need the same clothes and the same walk. A sunset drink on the seafront, a table booked in the old town, a look at the cruise ships in the harbour, a stroll past the boats in the marina. Each of them takes you along the promenade at the right hour and explains why you are dressed for an evening out. Avoid stories that need a car or a restaurant booking at a fixed time. A surprise falls apart when the other person asks why you are not heading for the taxi rank.</p><h2>How do you keep the time secret on the day?</h2><p>Give one time, the time to leave the hotel, and nothing else. People guess from details: a sunset hour, a booking email subject line, a "we need to be back by". Say "we leave at half past four" and leave it there.</p><p>If you need to tell us anything in the hours before, a dietary need, a running late message, use WhatsApp from your own phone. And if you would like a small touch on board, a particular bottle or a cake, ask us on WhatsApp before the day rather than turning up with it.</p><h2>What if the weather cancels the surprise?</h2><p>Have a plan B before you need one. Rough water is the one thing that can stop it. Should the skipper decide the water is too rough, the outing is cancelled, and you decide between a fresh date and your money back. You can also call it off yourself at no cost while there are still more than 24 hours to go. So book early in the holiday, keep an eye on the forecast two days out, and have a dinner in reserve that works as the evening on its own.</p><h2>When is a surprise boat trip a bad idea?</h2><p>When the person would not have said yes if asked. It depends on who they are:</p><ul><li><strong>They are afraid of deep water or of boats.</strong> A surprise turns a fear into a trap.</li><li><strong>They get seasick.</strong> Better to ask them, choose a calm morning and keep the trip short.</li><li><strong>They are pregnant, or have a back or mobility problem.</strong> These need a conversation, and sometimes a doctor, before anyone books.</li><li><strong>They hate surprises.</strong> Some people do. Tell them the day, keep the details secret.</li></ul><p>If what you are planning is a proposal, our guide to <a href="/posts/romantic-boat-trip-madeira-proposal-anniversary-guide">proposals and anniversaries at sea</a> is the place to start.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Can I book a boat trip in my name for someone else?</h3><p>Yes. Book in your own name and with your own phone number, so every confirmation and message about the trip comes to you.</p><h3>What time should we be at the marina for a surprise trip?</h3><p>Fifteen minutes ahead of the departure time, at the pontoon in Marina do Funchal, where your skipper will be waiting.</p><h3>What happens to the surprise if the sea is too rough?</h3><p>It moves. When the skipper rules the sea unsafe, the choice is a new date or all your money back, so keep a backup plan for that evening.</p><h3>Which trip is easiest to keep secret?</h3><p>The Sunset Trip, because it needs no swimwear and fits a cover story such as a drink or a dinner on the seafront.</p>

      <blockquote>Two clues only: wear flat shoes, leave at half past four.</blockquote>
    </article>
